(* `asif` PARSES AND IS REFUSED BY NAME, and it shares its production with
   `typed`, which is implemented. `type-annotation.tex:36-53` makes `asif` a
   type ASSUMPTION: it "considers both the static AND THE DYNAMIC type ... for
   the purposes of the immediately enclosing function, method, or operator
   invocation or field access", and the spec calls it a richer `super`.

   TREATING IT AS THE ASCRIPTION WOULD BE A SILENT WRONG ANSWER, not a missing
   feature: dispatch here is symmetric, whole-program and keyed on a concrete
   TAG, so `(self asif Generator).asString` would compile to the object's OWN
   method and print the wrong string. Refusing is the only honest reading until
   the dispatch half is built. *)

run(): () = do
    x: ZZ32 = (5 asif ZZ32)
    println(x)
end
